sic playing. He invokes VoiceBridge. Music automatically ducks. VoiceBridge interaction occurs. Music returns afterward.\nConcept:\nNormal state:\nMusic = normal volume\nDon invokes VoiceBridge:\nMusic ↓\nVoiceBridge active\nVoice interaction ends:\nMusic ↑ smoothly to prior level\nThis was enthusiastically accepted because it removes a constant ergonomic interruption.\nThe broader Voice Command Plane discussed previously was:\nVOICE\n  ↓\nINTERPRETER\n  ↓\nROUTER\n  ↓\nDETERMINISTIC EXECUTOR\n  ↓\nWINDOWS / GCE\nThe preferred control hierarchy remains:\nAPI / CLI\n>\nprocess/window control\n>\nkeyboard automation\n>\nmouse automation\nDucking should eventually use deterministic audio/session control rather than fake GUI clicking where possible.\n⸻\n6. MIXER CHANNEL CONCEPT\nNo permanent channel mapping has been ratified yet.\nThe concept is that physical channels could represent things such as:\nChannel 1 — Music\nChannel 2 — VoiceBridge\nChannel 3 — Main DevPad\nChannel 4 — Rookie DevPad\nChannel 5 — system / notifications\nChannel 6+ — contextual seat or runtime targets\nOr the same hardware could operate contextually rather than assigning every fader permanently.\nThe important rule:\nA physical channel is not inherently a seat.\nA fader is simply an input source.\nIts meaning comes from current orchestration context.\nThis avoids violating stable-seat doctrine by accidentally making hardware layout authoritative.\n⸻\n7. POTENTIAL PHYSICAL CONTROL SEMANTICS\nThese are conceptual mappings, not yet implementation authority.\nA mixer gives useful primitives:\nFader\nPossible meanings:\n* volume;\n* intensity;\n* priority;\n* context allocation;\n* resource weighting;\n* degree of engagement.\nRotary encoder / knob\nPossible meanings:\n* scroll;\n* model/runtime selection;\n* context-window navigation;\n* parameter selection;\n* zoom/detail level;\n* volume or gain.\nCue button\nHighly interesting for GCE.\nPossible meaning:\n“Prepare this target without making it a